From: OpenWiFi BCM4360 Project <bcm4360@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] brcmfmac: pcie: BCM4360 test.315 olmsg R3a NVRAM + pre-release doorbell

Fire #8 (0001-0014) passed CLEAN and brought the deepest evidence to
date: the host transport is CORRECT and the problem sits IN THE FW -- SB
intmask =0x00000000 (the fw never armed interrupts), ring rd_ptr=0 (the
fw never touched the ring), console stuck at 637 B. The boot statics
(the fw boot-stall analysis notes) pinpointed the stop in the
wlc_bmac_attach/PHY-descent cascade: OUR correct vars (boardtype=0x0552
from brcmfmac4360-pcie.txt) open the fw a path into the FullMAC D11/PHY
init that never ends. SB intmask=0 in fire #8 is the EFFECT of the
stop, not the cause -- the only intmask writer in the fw image is
pciedngl_intrs_on (the fw arms itself, after announcing bit 8, on the
dngl device-open path -- AFTER wl_probe returns). fw_init_done
(si+0x2028) has no writer in the image.

Kimptoc correction (the kimptoc test.28 comparison notes): in test.28
the ONLY moment in the WHOLE project when the fw answered the host with
doorbells (intstatus=0x300, mailboxint=0x03) was a PRE-RELEASE H2D
doorbell -- MAILBOXINT:=0xFFFFFFFF written in pcie_mask_irqs BEFORE
the ARM release, with the core in reset/halt. That fw had no NVRAM
(bmac_attach err 15 -> TRAP) and was BM-off, yet it lived on in
dngl/offload and sent 2 signals. A doorbell to a frozen WFI is a dead
path (our fires #6/#7 + kimptoc T279). The untested combination with a
positive precedent is therefore just one: R3a (NVRAM without boardtype,
the fw stays in dngl/offload, announces bit 8, arms intmask itself,
the olmsg channel gets an addressee -- BCM_OL pivot, full MAC on the
host) + P1 (a doorbell in the boot window, not to a WFI).

Patch 0015 (parameters bcm4360_test315 -- master switch,
bcm4360_test315_db_val and bcm4360_test315_no_master; test315=0 by
default -> inert patch -- zero behavioural difference, like all the
previous ones):

a) R3a: strip the "boardtype" variable from the NVRAM vars. The vars
   are NOT a table in pcie.c -- the real code: the vars buffer reaches
   brcmf_pcie_download_fw_nvram from brcmf_fw_nvram_strip (firmware.c)
   out of the brcmfmac4360-pcie.txt file; format: "key=value" entries
   NUL-terminated, zero-padded to a 4 B boundary, with a 4 B end token
   at the end (token = (~len/4)<<16 | len/4, firmware.c). The strip
   works IN PLACE at the download_fw_nvram entry -- BEFORE the two
   consumers: the TCM copy (address = rambase+ramsize-nvram_len, the
   token lands at TCM[ramsize-4]) and before the test276 snapshot
   (+0x020 varsz, +0x024..0x033 vars16). In-place compaction that
   drops the "boardtype=" entry, the remaining vars are preserved byte
   for byte, the pad and token are recomputed with the firmware.c
   formula; the buffer only shrinks (the parser buffer is larger than
   the stripped size). varsz shrinks, vars16 (the first 16 B =
   "sromrev=11\0boardt") stays sensible. fw without boardtype ->
   wlc_bmac_attach err 15 (prints "Unsupported Broadcom board type
   (0x%08x)", test.33) -> dngl/offload lives on: announces
   sbtopcimailbox=0x100 (the host sees BAR0+0x48 bit 8 -- the liveness
   gate WORKED), pciedngl_intrs_on arms SB intmask 0x100, the olmsg
   channel has an addressee (wl-split/BCM_OL).

b) P3: read-only MAILBOXMASK (BAR0+0x4C) read BEFORE
   brcmf_chip_set_active, with the ARM in halt (reset-state fw) --
   reproducing the kimptoc anomaly A0 (T284: readback 0x318
   pre-set_active, 0 post-set_active) on our substrate.

c) P1: between the shared_info write (test276 handshake) and
   brcmf_chip_set_active -- PLAIN write MAILBOXINT (BAR0+0x48) :=
   bcm4360_test315_db_val (default 0xFFFFFFFF = the exact kimptoc
   pattern from test.28; variants for later fires: 0x1 = TOSB_FN0_0,
   0x3 = FN0_0|FN0_1). This is NOT an RMW |= (like 0008/0013) -- a
   plain write, exactly as in kimptoc. pr_emerg bracketing before the
   write (the last line if the write kills the machine) and after the
   write + a control readback (class 0014(a): whether the write
   latched or was consumed).

d) DISCRIMINATOR: IMMEDIATELY AFTER brcmf_chip_set_active -- a group
   of single windowed reads bracketed (class
   T276-poll/T312-gate/0014-autopsy, proven on the live device in
   #6/#7): SB intstatus (BAR0+0x20), SB intmask (BAR0+0x24),
   MAILBOXINT (0x48), MAILBOXMASK (0x4C) + the fw console (test277
   mechanism: struct pointer from si[+0x010], wr_idx at struct+0x08),
   then ONE pr_emerg line "DISCRIMINATOR intstatus=0x.. intmask=0x..
   mbint=0x.. mbmask=0x.. console_wr=0x..". The deciding read of the
   whole fire #9, runs ALWAYS (before the test.312 gate and before any
   0012/0013 doorbell). Success discriminator: intstatus ~0x300
   (latched D2H doorbell bits -- kimptoc test.28) and/or intmask 0x100
   (pciedngl_intrs_on -- the only fw-side writer) and/or wr_idx
   >0x27d (637). Failure: intmask 0 + wr_idx 0x27d = the frozen WFI of
   fires #6-#8. An invalid console pointer (0 at t~0 -- the fw
   publishes it ~10 ms later) skips only the second TCM read; the
   DISCRIMINATOR line always prints.

e) P4: bcm4360_test315_no_master=1 -- skip pci_set_master (chip 4360
   only, the master switch is required) -- control for whether the fw
   reaction needs bus mastering/DMA (kimptoc test.28 ran BM-off).

f) Gate 0012 untouched. test313 (doorbell-anyway) is explicitly NOT
   part of this test: on a gate timeout (fw still frozen) the path
   goes into the T309 hold -- that IS the correct test answer (a
   doorbell to a frozen WFI is a dead path, fire #7 + T279). test314
   stays a separate parameter: fire #9 will set test314=1 so the
   autopsy runs if the gate passed and rxctl died.

Safety (imposed by wedges #4/#5): all new MMIO operations are single
windowed reads/writes (zero loops), each bracketed by pr_emerg before/
after (pattern test.310/311/314); the post-release BAR0 read class =
T276-poll/T312-gate/0014 (proven live in #6/#7), the pre-release BAR0
class = experiment #1 fire 2 (T305 MAILBOXMASK write + readback
pre-set_active, WRITE_LANDED). R3a is pure CPU (host RAM, zero MMIO).
ONE new MMIO write = pre-release doorbell (only with test315=1) -- in
the window where the ARM is in halt (safest moment; kimptoc did it in a
full release cycle). test315=0 (default) => zero code and behavioural
difference (the new parameters are read solely under the master switch,
default 0 -> all ifs false). T309, gate 0012 and test314 untouched
(nothing added in teardown).

Requires: test276=1 + test308=1 (full fire #9 pipeline); fire only with
setpci ASPM-off + CTO 10-100 ms on the live device.

~250 lines, gated on chip 4360 + test315 -- zero behaviour change for
every other chip and for the msgbuf path.

